Partial fix (confirmed real, confirmed INSUFFICIENT ALONE -- see
RESULTS-restore-corruption-4.md): the Mamba/GDN conv-state window's
reserved speculative-decode rows carry stale, uninitialized bytes into
offloaded checkpoints. This closes that specific gap, verified by direct
byte-scan of stored chunks (the previously-universal ~61KB scribble-
survival signature in every Mamba layer is now gone). It does NOT by
itself close `test_kv_cache_identity_corruption.py`'s repro -- the
scribble star-gate test (RESTORE_CORRUPTION_PLAN4.md) still corrupts
after this patch. A second, separate unwritten-region bug (a request's
very first offloaded chunk, across ALL groups including attention, not
just Mamba) is documented as the likely remaining cause in
RESULTS-restore-corruption-4.md's Anomalies section -- not yet fixed.
Keep this patch (it is a correct, verified fix for the bug it targets)
but do not mark the overall restore-corruption investigation closed on
its account alone.

Full investigation across four sessions: RESTORE_CORRUPTION_PLAN.md
through PLAN4.md, RESULTS-restore-corruption.md through -4.md (this
patch's own session). Repro: `venv/bin/python
test_kv_cache_identity_corruption.py --convs 3 --rounds 2` (a conversation
primed alongside another, then evicted and restored, decodes token soup
from the first token; deterministic).

Root cause, verified by direct byte-scan of stored offload chunks
(scan_kv_chunks.py) and code trace (v1/worker/mamba_utils.py,
model_executor/layers/mamba/mamba_utils.py):

Each Mamba/GDN ("linear_attn") layer's per-block conv-state tensor has
`conv_kernel_size - 1 + num_speculative_blocks` window rows
(MambaStateShapeCalculator.gated_delta_net_state_shape) -- for this
deployment (conv_kernel_size=4, 3 MTP draft tokens), 6 rows: 3 "confirmed"
history rows plus 3 reserved for draft-token conv history during
decode-time speculative verification. preprocess_mamba's boundary-
crossing precopy (the `_copy_mamba_state_block` conv branch,
`state[dst, :conv_width - token_bias]`) is a *copy*, not a fresh write:
during plain prefill `accept_token_bias` is always 0 (reset to 1 after
every crossing, `num_accepted_tokens_cpu[i] = 1`), so the copy width
covers the *entire* window including the 3 reserved rows -- but it only
ever carries forward whatever the source block already held there.
Nothing, anywhere in the prefill path, ever writes fresh data into those
rows; they are simply propagated block-to-block, chunk to chunk, all the
way back to a request's very first block, whose reserved rows are exactly
whatever that physical GPU block's allocation happened to contain: zero
on a virgin (CUDA-zeroed) page, or a *different, unrelated conversation's*
stale conv-window bytes on a block recycled from the pool.

Confirmed directly:
- scan_kv_chunks.py, run against a stored chunk file from an isolated
  (uncontended) priming with every KV-cache tensor deliberately filled
  with a nonzero pattern right after allocation
  (patches/restore-corruption-scribble-debug.patch,
  VLLM_DEBUG_SCRIBBLE_KV=big): every one of 48 Mamba layer instances (3
  groups x 16 layers) shows the scribble pattern surviving at byte offset
  61,440, length 61,440 -- exactly the last 3 of the 6 conv-window rows
  (3 rows x 10,240 elements x 2 bytes/bfloat16 = 61,440 bytes) -- in every
  chunk except the very first. The attention group (17 layers, including
  the MTP drafter's own `mtp.layers.0.self_attn.attn`) shows zero
  surviving bytes: fully written, not implicated.
- Control: the same scan against an *unscribbled* isolated priming's
  stored chunks reads exactly that same byte range as zero, confirming
  it is genuinely never written, not an artifact of the scribble
  methodology.
- Since a conversation primed entirely alone (nothing else ever sharing
  the GPU pool) always restores clean, while the identical conversation
  primed alongside others reliably corrupts on restore (four sessions of
  A/B testing, RESULTS-restore-corruption.md through -3.md), the
  mechanism is exactly this: c0-on-an-empty-pool's reserved rows are
  zero (harmless); a later conversation's reserved rows inherit a
  recycled block's stale bytes from whichever conversation used that
  physical block before (harmful) -- deterministic garbage in,
  deterministic token-soup out, matching the byte-identical corrupted
  output observed run after run, session after session, for the same
  conversation content.

Not H-MTP (RESTORE_CORRUPTION_PLAN4.md's leading hypothesis going in):
the MTP draft attention layer's own KV is fully written, every chunk,
every layer -- ruled out by the same scan. Also not a data-movement bug:
RESULTS-restore-corruption.md/-2.md already established the stored bytes
travel store -> RAM -> load -> GPU-read byte-faithfully, for both clean
and corrupted restores; this patch closes the remaining gap -- the bytes
being faithfully carried were wrong from the moment of capture, and this
is why.

Fix: `_zero_conv_speculative_reserve()` in `preprocess_mamba()`
(v1/worker/mamba_utils.py) explicitly zeros the trailing
`num_speculative_blocks` conv-window rows of the *destination* block on
every boundary-crossing precopy -- the one point every block passes
through before becoming "the current checkpoint," whether freshly
allocated or recycled from the pool. This makes the content
deterministic (matching the already-proven-safe virgin-page behavior)
regardless of the physical block's history, for every group with
`num_speculative_blocks > 0` (a no-op otherwise -- no reserved rows
exist without a draft config). Safe during genuine decode-time
speculation: the reserved rows are pure scratch *output* for the current
step's forward pass (each draft position's conv state is computed
sequentially, within that same step, from the confirmed rows plus this
step's own draft tokens -- never read as input before this step writes
it), so zeroing them immediately beforehand cannot destroy anything the
kernel depends on.

Verification: see RESULTS-restore-corruption-4.md for the full gate
results run against this patch.
--- a/v1/worker/mamba_utils.py	2026-08-24 15:00:08.984392599 +0000
+++ b/v1/worker/mamba_utils.py	2026-08-24 15:36:52.554523714 +0000
@@ -1106,6 +1106,74 @@
             )
 
 
+def _zero_conv_speculative_reserve(
+    mamba_group_ids: list[int],
+    num_speculative_blocks: int,
+    dest_block_idx: int,
+    kv_cache_config: KVCacheConfig,
+    mamba_state_copy_funcs: tuple[MambaStateCopyFunc, ...],
+    req_state: CachedRequestState,
+    forward_context: dict[str, Any],
+) -> None:
+    """Fix for the KV-offload restore-corruption bug (docs/kv-offload-
+    cache-identity-corruption.md, RESTORE_CORRUPTION_PLAN4.md).
+
+    The conv-state window has `conv_kernel_size - 1 + num_speculative_blocks`
+    rows (get_state_shape() / MambaStateShapeCalculator.gated_delta_net_
+    state_shape); the trailing `num_speculative_blocks` rows exist to hold
+    draft-token conv history during decode-time speculative verification.
+    Verified by direct byte-scan (scan_kv_chunks.py) and code trace: during
+    plain prefill nothing ever writes those rows. preprocess_mamba's own
+    precopy (above) is a *copy* -- at token_bias=0 (the prefill case) it
+    covers the full window including these rows, but it only ever carries
+    forward whatever the source block already had. A request's very first
+    block's reserved rows are therefore whatever that physical block's
+    allocation happened to hold: zero on a virgin GPU page (harmless, and
+    why locally-resident conversations and c0-on-an-empty-pool never showed
+    this), a *different, unrelated conversation's* stale conv history on a
+    block recycled from the pool (not harmless -- confirmed by the
+    VLLM_DEBUG_SCRIBBLE_KV scribble test: filling every KV-cache tensor
+    with garbage right after allocation turned a previously always-clean
+    isolated-priming-then-restore into a reliably corrupted one). That
+    stale content silently rides along into any offloaded checkpoint and
+    corrupts the very first decode step of whatever request later restores
+    it, deterministically (same stale bytes in, same garbled tokens out).
+
+    Zeroing here -- the one point every block passes through before
+    becoming "the current checkpoint," whether freshly allocated or
+    recycled -- makes the content deterministic (matching the already-
+    proven-safe virgin-page behavior) regardless of the physical block's
+    history. Safe during genuine decode-time speculation too: the reserved
+    rows are pure scratch *output* for the current step's forward pass --
+    each draft position's conv state is computed sequentially within that
+    same step from the confirmed rows plus this step's own draft tokens,
+    never read as input before this step writes it. Zeroing them
+    immediately before the forward pass runs cannot destroy anything the
+    kernel depends on.
+
+    No-op when num_speculative_blocks == 0 (no MTP/EAGLE draft config):
+    the conv window then has no reserved rows at all.
+    """
+    for gid in mamba_group_ids:
+        block_ids = req_state.block_ids[gid]
+        if dest_block_idx >= len(block_ids):
+            continue
+        block_id = block_ids[dest_block_idx]
+        layer_names = kv_cache_config.kv_cache_groups[gid].layer_names
+        for layer_name in layer_names:
+            attention = forward_context.get(layer_name)
+            if attention is None:
+                continue
+            kv_caches: list[torch.Tensor] = attention.kv_cache
+            for state, copy_func in zip(kv_caches, mamba_state_copy_funcs):
+                if copy_func is not get_conv_copy_spec:
+                    continue
+                if is_conv_state_dim_first():
+                    state[block_id, :, -num_speculative_blocks:].zero_()
+                else:
+                    state[block_id, -num_speculative_blocks:, :].zero_()
+
+
